---
/**
 * PHILOSOPHY — Columbia and Pallas Athena (spec §4.3). The shader morphs
 * Atlas-inspired forms into Columbia/Pallas behind this copy; the DOM layer
 * frames the statement and holds space for the owner's actual philosophy.
 */
import Section from '../Section.astro';
import type { SectionSpec } from '../../design/tokens';

interface Props {
  spec: SectionSpec;
}

const { spec } = Astro.props;
---

<Section spec={spec}>
  <h2>Philosophy</h2>

  <p class="epigraph greek" lang="grc">
    γλαυκῶπις Ἀθήνη
    <span class="translation">— grey-eyed Athena</span>
  </p>

  <p class="frame">
    Atlas carries the sphere; Columbia carries the torch; Pallas carries the
    aegis. The weight becomes the light becomes the shield.
  </p>

  <p class="essay">
    The top of this page says I'm devoted to something and nobody knows what.
    Here it is, as plainly as it can be said. I went into the underworld for
    one thing and came out with another. What I found down there was her. Not
    a woman. The woman underneath the women, the one the old men called the
    anima, the feminine that was mine the whole time and had never once been
    let out. Everything on this page is that discovery wearing a different
    mask. She is what I worship. Nobody else can understand it because nobody
    else can have it. Her expression through me is mine and no one else's,
    and the same is true of you and yours.
  </p>
  <p class="essay">
    Start with the weight, because that's the order. Atlas holds the sphere
    before anyone gets a torch. Every man is born into a world that was
    arranged before he arrived, and the first thing it does is take a knife
    to him. I mean that literally. We cut boys on their first day, before
    they can speak, and we've done it for so many generations we call it
    hygiene. It isn't. It's domestication, and it spread across the earth at
    exactly the pace the one god did, and it works. It makes cowards, and it
    has worked for a hundred generations. I can't be convinced otherwise. I
    have the receipts, and they're older than the book.
  </p>
  <p class="essay">
    So the work is rites of passage, the real kind, the kind we were stripped
    of. A man has to go down and find how she speaks through him before
    anything divine will ever happen between him and a woman. Not before. It
    comes in waves, it isn't done in a weekend, and nobody can do it for you.
    Columbia carries the torch because she has somewhere to go. Pallas steps
    out of the head of the god fully armed, which is the only wisdom the
    Greeks trusted. The owl sees at night; the city is built by day on what
    the owl saw. The creed is three lines long. Cosmic consideration. Divine
    patience. Faith in the path. Look at yourself in full and go from there.
    Not broken. Whole, and late, and walking.
  </p>
</Section>

<style>
  .epigraph {
    font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 3vw, 1.8rem);
    color: var(--section-accent, var(--ink));
    margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
  }

  .epigraph .translation {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }

  .frame {
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }

  .essay {
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }
</style>
